1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,250
English, the same as every form I ever
filled in at the plant. Nothing here to
think about.

2
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:00,500
Not somebody else's. If I am going to
write down honestly what I can and cannot
do, I fill in every line of it myself.

3
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:00,750
Right. Start at the top and work down, the
way you fill in any form that matters.

4
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:01,000
It wants a name for the place. The one it
offered is somewhere in the middle of the
country and this is not that.

5
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:01,250
Fern Creek. Two miles of it either side of
the river, the mill at the north end, my
rooms on the top floor of a two-family
house.

6
00:00:01,250 --> 00:00:01,500
The creek is the first thing I would tell
anybody about this town.

7
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:01,750
It comes down out of the woods behind the
high school.

8
00:00:01,750 --> 00:00:02,000
It goes under the road twice on its way
through.

9
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:02,250
And then into the river a quarter mile
below the mill.

10
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:02,500
My father fished it every April, when the
water was still too cold to be pleasant
about.

11
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:02,750
He taught me to stand still and let the
line do the work.

12
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:03,000
That is the only thing he taught me that I
have never once managed to apply anywhere
else.

13
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:03,250
Not today either. Today I want water I can
drink, not water I can stand in.

14
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:03,500
There. Where I am, written down, so the
log starts with a place and not a feeling.

15
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:03,750
Accepted. I am not going to argue with the
two settings underneath it either; they
are what they are, and so is the week I
have had.

16
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:04,000
Finish. Now the part that actually
matters, which is what I can do and what I
cannot.

17
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:04,250
Yes. I am sure. Nothing on that page was
going to be improved by me looking at it
for another ten minutes.

18
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:04,500
Friends to the end. I had colleagues, and
I sent them to the buses. There is a
difference between those two words and I
have had five days to think about it.

19
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:04,750
Hunted forever. No. Whatever is out there
has not been following me. It has been
standing where it was left.

20
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:05,000
A helicopter crash. Nobody I have ever met
has been in one of those.

21
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:05,250
Infected. I have looked at my arms twice
an hour for five days and there is nothing
on them.

22
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:05,500
Prison. The nearest one is forty miles
south and I have never once been inside
it.

23
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:05,750
Sheltered. That is the same roof as the
first entry, with the door still shut and
somebody else behind it.

24
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:06,000
Surrounded. Not yet. I have been careful
and I have been lucky, and I know exactly
which of the two has done more of the
work.

25
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:06,250
Hunted again. Whoever wrote this list has
a favourite.

26
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:06,500
A burning building. I spent that whole
afternoon making certain this would not be
anybody's.

27
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:06,750
Bordered in. I would not have got out of
the state on those roads that afternoon
even if I had left at six with everybody
else.

28
00:00:06,750 --> 00:00:07,000
A party. On a Tuesday, at my age, two
miles from a live plant. No.

29
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:07,250
A car crash. My truck is still in the lot
at work with three quarters of a tank in
it, because the road out was solid metal
by four o'clock and I walked.

30
00:00:07,250 --> 00:00:07,500
Hunted by the dead. Three entries on this
page say the same thing in different
words, and not one of them is what
happened to me.

31
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:07,750
A squatter. Not yet. Ask me again tonight,
when I have to pick a room with a door.

32
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:08,000
A large building. I have been in exactly
one large building all week and it had a
boiler house attached to it.

33
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:08,250
Low profile. That is a description of how
I intend to spend today. It is not a
description of how I got here.

34
00:00:08,250 --> 00:00:08,500
Mass quarantine. There was no cordon on
this side of the river. There was a list,
a time, and a school car park.

35
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:08,750
A refugee centre. That is where the buses
were going, and I have no idea at all
whether they arrived.

36
00:00:08,750 --> 00:00:09,000
Missed. There it is, one word, and it is
the honest one. Not brave, not chosen, not
unlucky. I was thirty feet from a drum
gauge with two operators watching me when
the last bus left the school.

37
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:09,250
Take it. Stubbornness, it says, or
ignorance, or bad luck. I will own the
first one and argue about the other two
another day.

38
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:09,500
Before I spend anything I want to see the
ledger it is keeping. Twenty-two years of
budgets taught me that much.

39
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:09,750
The one it starts on says no limits are
enforced. I have never in my life been
handed a job with no limits enforced and I
am not going to pretend today is that.

40
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:10,000
Three separate budgets: what I am made of,
what is wrong with me, and what I know how
to do. Stat money can go downhill into the
other two and never back up. That is a
ledger I recognise.

41
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:10,250
It has me down as a man. I am not, and the
rest of this form is going to be accurate
whether or not that matters to anybody but
me.

42
00:00:10,250 --> 00:00:10,500
The first key I tried did nothing to it.
Fine. There is always a second way in, and
finding it is most of the job.

43
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:10,750
Leave the sex field for a moment. The name
I can do from memory.

44
00:00:10,750 --> 00:00:11,000
Delphine. My grandmother's name. She came
down from Quebec at fifteen to sew uppers
in a shoe shop.

45
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:11,250
She never once in her life let anybody see
her cry in a kitchen.

46
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:11,500
I have not managed that. I have managed
most of the rest of it.

47
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:11,750
Everybody who ever paid me called me Del.

48
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:12,000
Two syllables was more than the floor of
that mill ever had time for.

49
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:12,250
There is nobody left within twenty miles
to shorten it now.

50
00:00:12,250 --> 00:00:12,500
So it goes down in full.

51
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:12,750
Delphine.

52
00:00:12,750 --> 00:00:13,000
Then the other half, which is my father's.

53
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:13,250
Ouellette. It is on the pension paperwork
at the plant in his own handwriting.

54
00:00:13,250 --> 00:00:13,500
He was a millwright in that building
before the strike years.

55
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:13,750
He taught me to listen to a machine before
I looked at it.

56
00:00:13,750 --> 00:00:14,000
To keep my own tools clean.

57
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:14,250
And to fish the river in the spring, when
the water is still too cold to be pleasant
about it.

58
00:00:14,250 --> 00:00:14,500
Two of those three are going to matter
before dark.

59
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:14,750
The tools are in a red steel box on a
bench two miles from here.

60
00:00:14,750 --> 00:00:15,000
Which is the joke of my life, just at the
minute.

61
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:15,250
There. All of it, spelled the way she
spelled it.

62
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:15,500
Accepted. Delphine Ouellette, forty-seven
years old, of Mount Chase, Maine, and this
is the fifth morning.

63
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:15,750
Stop guessing and read the instructions.
That is not humility, it is just faster.

64
00:00:15,750 --> 00:00:16,000
There it is in black and white. The sex
field wants the at sign, and the key I
thought was doing nothing was moving the
cursor down the list instead. Noted. Close
it.

65
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:16,250
Now the field itself. I am a woman of
forty-seven and the form is going to say
so before I put my name on the bottom of
it.

66
00:00:16,250 --> 00:00:16,500
And the clothes to match, since the boots
and the shirt are mine and not borrowed
off a shelf.

67
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:16,750
Age next. There is no point being coy
about it: the number is half the reason my
back is the way it is.

68
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:17,000
It has guessed me twenty-four, which is
generous and useless. Forty-seven.

69
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:17,250
Forty-seven. Twenty-two of them in the
same building, and I would not give those
back.

70
00:00:17,250 --> 00:00:17,500
Accepted.

71
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:17,750
Forty-seven, and the field took it without
comment. On to the work.

72
00:00:17,750 --> 00:00:18,000
Past the start I already chose. What I did
for a living is the next line and it is
the one I can answer without thinking.

73
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:18,250
The list of what people did for a living
is a mile long and I already know my line
on it. Search for it instead.

74
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:18,500
Mechanical.

75
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:18,750
Not electrical, not chemical, not the
software people on the second floor.

76
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:19,000
Pumps, fans, gearboxes, bearings, headers,
valves.

77
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:19,250
Anything that turns, anything under
pressure, anything that gets hot.

78
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:19,500
Twenty-two years of it in one building.

79
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:19,750
And the whole steam side of that building
was mine by the end.

80
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:20,000
Not because I asked for it. Because
everyone else who understood it left.

81
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:20,250
Which is how you become the one they phone
at three in the morning.

82
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:20,500
I never once let it ring out.

83
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:20,750
Mechanical. There.

84
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:21,000
Show me what is left.

85
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:21,250
One entry left and it is mine. Mechanical
engineer, and it costs me two out of the
skills budget, which is the cheapest
honest thing on this whole form.

86
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:21,500
There is a tab for what I did with my
evenings. It can wait; the body comes
first.

87
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:21,750
Four numbers for what I am made of.
Thirty-eight points to spend on them and
no way to get any of it back afterwards.

88
00:00:21,750 --> 00:00:22,000
Strength first. I have swung a two-foot
pipe wrench at a seized coupling more
mornings than I can count.

89
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:22,250
Ten. Above average, it says, and that is
right: strong for a woman my age and no
use pretending further.

90
00:00:22,250 --> 00:00:22,500
That is what I can lift. What I cannot do
with it comes later on the trait page.

91
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:22,750
And here is where I pay for it. I have
never been quick. I plan instead, and when
a plan runs out I am the wrong woman to be
standing there.

92
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:23,000
Seven. Below average, and I would rather
the form said so than flattered me.

93
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:23,250
This is the one I am actually good at.

94
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:23,500
Give me a machine and I will tell you what
is wrong with it, usually before the cover
is off.

95
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:23,750
That is not a boast. It is twenty-two
years of being the one who had to know.

96
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:24,000
Twelve. Top tenth, it reckons. A head full
of machines, which is all I have brought
with me.

97
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:24,250
One point left over and one line to spend
it on.

98
00:00:24,250 --> 00:00:24,500
My eyes still read a gauge from across a
bay and my ears are the best thing I own.
Both of those live on this line.

99
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:24,750
Ten, seven, twelve, nine, and nothing left
over. Now the part I would rather write
down myself than be surprised by later.

100
00:00:24,750 --> 00:00:25,000
Liabilities first. Nothing on the good
side of this page is affordable until I
have owned up to the bad.

101
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:25,250
The back.

102
00:00:25,250 --> 00:00:25,500
Six years ago, on a lift I should have
refused and did not.

103
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:25,750
One end of a gearbox casing, a man off
sick, and a shift that was already late.

104
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:26,000
I felt it go and I finished the job
anyway, which is the whole of my
personality in one afternoon.

105
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:26,250
It has never once been right since.

106
00:00:26,250 --> 00:00:26,500
Find it.

107
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:26,750
Bad back. Thirty-five per cent off what I
can carry, and it pays me three points for
the privilege of being honest about it. I
can still lift. I cannot carry. That is
going to decide what I pick up and what I
walk away from, all day.

108
00:00:26,750 --> 00:00:27,000
Next. The sleep.

109
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:27,250
Not since the winter my mother was dying
and I lived in a chair beside her for four
months.

110
00:00:27,250 --> 00:00:27,500
Two hours a night. Two more if I am owed a
favour.

111
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:27,750
Other people lie down and go under like a
thrown switch.

112
00:00:27,750 --> 00:00:28,000
I lie there and listen to the building and
wait to be tired enough to be allowed in.

113
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:28,250
Tonight, whenever tonight comes, that is
going to matter more than anything else on
this page.

114
00:00:28,250 --> 00:00:28,500
Find it.

115
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:28,750
Insomniac. A hard time falling asleep even
under the best circumstances, it says, and
it hands me two points for saying so out
loud.

116
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:29,000
One more. The legs.

117
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:29,250
Slow. I have never been quick and I am
slower now than I was at forty.

118
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:29,500
Fifteen per cent off my pace on flat
ground, and there is nothing flat about a
street with cars across it.

119
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:29,750
Running is not a plan available to me. It
has not been for years.

120
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:30,000
So every room I go into, I want to know
the way out of before I am in it.

121
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:30,250
That is not caution. That is arithmetic.

122
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:30,500
Three liabilities, eight points, and every
one of them true.

123
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:30,750
Find it.

124
00:00:30,750 --> 00:00:31,000
Slow footed. Three points back, and the
plain truth of what my legs are worth on a
street I have to leave in a hurry.

125
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:31,250
Eight points in the pot, all of it
borrowed against my own body. Now what it
bought.

126
00:00:31,250 --> 00:00:31,500
The ears.

127
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:31,750
I can hear a bad bearing through a
concrete floor.

128
00:00:31,750 --> 00:00:32,000
That is not a party trick, it is the whole
of preventive maintenance.

129
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:32,250
And in five days I have learned it works
on rooms as well as on gearboxes.

130
00:00:32,250 --> 00:00:32,500
I can hear which ones have something in
them long before there is anything to see.

131
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:32,750
It has kept me alive twice already and I
did not have to be brave either time.

132
00:00:32,750 --> 00:00:33,000
Find it.

133
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:33,250
Good hearing, one point. Cheapest thing on
the page and the most useful thing I own.

134
00:00:33,250 --> 00:00:33,500
And one more.

135
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:33,750
The hands.

136
00:00:33,750 --> 00:00:34,000
Twenty-two years of threading nuts into
holes I could not see.

137
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:34,250
They come back from a slip before my head
has caught up.

138
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:34,500
Cuts close, knuckles heal, and I have
never once needed stitches from that
building.

139
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:34,750
It is the one part of me that has not aged
at the rate the rest has.

140
00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:35,000
Two points, and worth it: I am going to be
handling broken glass and torn metal all
day.

141
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:35,250
Find it.

142
00:00:35,250 --> 00:00:35,500
Fast healer, two points. Five liabilities
and boons between them, three points spent
against eight earned, and I am five to the
good with the skills page still to pay
for.

143
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:35,750
Five points left over from being honest
about myself. Now what I did with my
evenings.

144
00:00:35,750 --> 00:00:36,000
Because a working life is not the only
place a person learns anything.

145
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:36,250
The honest one first, again.

146
00:00:36,250 --> 00:00:36,500
There is nothing in my shirt pocket but
the pocket.

147
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:36,750
I quit twice in my thirties and failed
twice.

148
00:00:36,750 --> 00:00:37,000
One hour without and I am mean. Two and I
cannot hold a thought in a straight line.

149
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:37,250
I know precisely how the rest of that
goes, and there is no shop between here
and the river that has not already been
emptied.

150
00:00:37,250 --> 00:00:37,500
So it is going to be a hard day, and none
of it will be the day's fault.

151
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:37,750
Find it.

152
00:00:37,750 --> 00:00:38,000
Nicotine dependence. It earns me a point,
which is the only good thing the habit has
ever done for me.

153
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:38,250
Now the useful evenings.

154
00:00:38,250 --> 00:00:38,500
The mill sent us to the first-aid
refresher every spring.

155
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:38,750
Most people slept through it. I paid
attention every single year.

156
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:39,000
Because a man went into a nip point on the
third floor in ninety-nine and nobody on
that shift knew what to do with him.

157
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:39,250
I can stop a bleed, splint a wrist, and
tell the difference between a wound that
is frightening and one that is killing
you.

158
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:39,500
Today that is worth more than anything
else I know.

159
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:39,750
Find it.

160
00:00:39,750 --> 00:00:40,000
First aid, one point. I would pay five.

161
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:40,250
And the river.

162
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:40,500
My father taught me to fish it in April.

163
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:40,750
Which sounds like sentiment and is not: it
is how you find water and read weather
coming up a valley.

164
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:41,000
And how you learn not to eat something
stupid because it looked all right.

165
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:41,250
There is a creek in this town and I am
going to need it before the day is out.

166
00:00:41,250 --> 00:00:41,500
Find it.

167
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:41,750
Fishing, one point, and the outdoorsman
that comes free with it. Being wet is
going to be part of today.

168
00:00:41,750 --> 00:00:42,000
One more that is really just the day job
spilling over.

169
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:42,250
Machinist.

170
00:00:42,250 --> 00:00:42,500
Half the work in an old plant is that the
part you want is not in the crib.

171
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:42,750
So you build one out of whatever is on the
bench.

172
00:00:42,750 --> 00:00:43,000
A bracket, a wedge, a handle, a length of
stock jammed across the inside of a
window.

173
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:43,250
That last one I am going to want tonight.

174
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:43,500
Find it.

175
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:43,750
Machinist, beginner, two points.
Fabrication and a bit more mechanics on
top of what the job already gave me.

176
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:44,000
And the last one, which is only on here
because it is true.

177
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:44,250
I drive. Or I did.

178
00:00:44,250 --> 00:00:44,500
Down through a dead town at three in the
morning more times than I can count, with
the heater going and nobody on the road.

179
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:44,750
My truck is in the lot at work and I am
not going to see it again.

180
00:00:44,750 --> 00:00:45,000
But if I find something with keys in it
and a clear road, I can move it, and that
costs nothing to write down.

181
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:45,250
Find it.

182
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:45,500
The licence is already there. Leave it
alone and keep going.

183
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:45,750
The bad back, the sleeplessness, and the
slow feet are all debts I already know.

184
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:46,000
Last, the things I can still do when
somebody gets close enough that a tool is
not enough.

185
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:46,250
A hammer, a crowbar, a length of pipe—if I
have to swing one, I need to know where
the weight goes.

186
00:00:46,250 --> 00:00:46,500
An edge can cut clean, but I have spent my
life with blunt steel and handles.

187
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:46,750
Getting out of the way matters more than
looking brave.

188
00:00:46,750 --> 00:00:47,000
Any of it starts with footing, distance,
and not freezing.

189
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:47,250
Two hard lessons: keep my balance, and
strike only when I mean it.

190
00:00:47,250 --> 00:00:47,500
That is enough. I know what I know, and
there is nothing left to pretend.

191
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:47,750
The numbers close. The life on the page is
mine, debts and all. I am done choosing.

192
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:48,000
Yes. This is me. Find a door that closes,
find water, find one good wrench, and keep
breathing.

193
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:48,250
Before I take a single step, I need one
quiet breath and a clear beginning.

194
00:00:48,250 --> 00:00:48,500
Restaurant. Eight in the morning. Too many
dead to the west. I fix that in my head
before I move.

195
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:48,750
English again. Then I can return to the
life waiting here.

196
00:00:48,750 --> 00:00:49,000
Fern Creek. Open the only life I have
here.

197
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:49,250
Delphine Ouellette. Back into it.

198
00:00:49,250 --> 00:00:49,500
East through the restaurant. Move before
they close in.

199
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:50,500
I see them around the building. East is
still my way through.

200
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:50,750
Go east now. Tables and booths are better
than the street.

201
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:51,000
Another step east, over the seating if I
must.

202
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:52,000
More of them come into view, still
distant. I cannot stop on this bench.

203
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:52,250
Off the bench and east. Keep the tables
between me and the doors.

204
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:54,250
East across the floor. Do not waste the
distance I have.

205
00:00:54,250 --> 00:00:54,500
Keep the ground I gained. The distant dead
can stay behind me.

206
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:55,500
Keep east through the dining room. The
noise stays behind me.

207
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:55,750
Keep the step. Distant movement is no
reason to turn back.

208
00:00:55,750 --> 00:00:56,750
Another step east. Keep the walls and
furniture working for me.

209
00:00:56,750 --> 00:00:57,000
Keep moving. What I can see is still far
enough away.

210
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,000
East again. I need the far side of this
room.

211
00:00:59,000 --> 00:00:59,250
Keep the step. Nothing near me is worth
turning around for.

212
00:00:59,250 --> 00:01:00,250
Keep east. The open floor is buying me
time.

213
00:01:00,250 --> 00:01:01,250
Stay on this ground. Keep the retreat
moving east.

214
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:02,250
The next tile is clear. Keep east.

215
00:01:02,250 --> 00:01:03,250
Another clear tile east. Keep going.

216
00:01:03,250 --> 00:01:05,250
Keep east toward the far wall.

217
00:01:05,250 --> 00:01:05,500
East again. The far wall is close now.

218
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:05,750
An alarm is sounding east. Stopping on
this bench would be worse.

219
00:01:05,750 --> 00:01:06,750
More shapes northeast and southwest, still
distant. Keep moving.

220
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:07,000
East again. Keep the noise and the crowd
behind me.

221
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Glass is breaking ahead. A damaged way out
is still a way out.

222
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Keep this step. Whatever is north is not
close enough to make me retreat.

223
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:10,250
The floor ahead is open. Keep east.

224
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:10,500
The glass is crunching east of me. Keep
moving toward it.

225
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:11,500
More of them are visible, but none are
beside me. Keep moving.

226
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:12,500
East now. I still have room to move.

227
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:14,500
Keep east. The nearest one is still across
the room.

228
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:15,500
Try the next tile east. I need the outer
wall.

229
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:16,500
Off this bench. The glass wall is close.

230
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:16,750
East again. The crashes are closer, but
the path is still open.

231
00:01:16,750 --> 00:01:17,750
Curtains and glass block the east wall. I
need a hole.

232
00:01:17,750 --> 00:01:18,000
Drive a knee through the glass. Make the
opening.

233
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:18,250
The glass is gone. Step into the frame.

234
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:18,500
I see them. Keep safe mode on, but let me
use the opening.

235
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:22,500
Now step into the smashed frame.

236
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:22,750
Yes. Through before they close the
distance.

237
00:01:22,750 --> 00:01:27,750
Something is close to the northeast. Out
through the frame, then away.

238
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:28,000
Keep the step. The warning is west, not in
front of me.

239
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:28,250
One is right above me. South, down the
alley.

240
00:01:28,250 --> 00:01:29,250
I see the one beside the alley. I need the
south step.

241
00:01:29,250 --> 00:01:29,500
South now. Put the wall between me and
that reach.

242
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:30,500
Growling behind me. One more step south.

243
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:30,750
Keep the south step. The tougher one is
northwest, not in my path.

244
00:01:30,750 --> 00:01:31,750
The brick east of me is solid. One more
step south toward the open window.

245
00:01:31,750 --> 00:01:32,000
Keep the south step. The open window is
southeast of me now.

246
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,000
There it is, open to the southeast.
Through.

247
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:35,250
Keep the window step. The house is still
safer than the alley.

248
00:01:35,250 --> 00:01:39,250
One is northeast of me. Cut southeast into
the room.

249
00:01:39,250 --> 00:01:39,500
Keep the step. Going back would put me in
the window again.

250
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:39,750
Two of them are beside me. I need to run
now.

251
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:40,000
Run. Put the room between us.

252
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:40,250
Run southeast onto the clear floor. Do not
let them close around me.

253
00:01:40,250 --> 00:01:41,250
I am running now, but safe mode caught the
step. I see them. Southeast.

254
00:01:41,250 --> 00:01:42,250
Southeast now. Use the open lane.

255
00:01:42,250 --> 00:01:42,500
They are north of me. Run east along the
open floor.

256
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:43,500
One is just northwest of me. Keep running
east.

257
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:43,750
The gap is opening. Keep east while I
still have breath.

258
00:01:43,750 --> 00:01:44,750
The floor stays clear. Keep running east.

259
00:01:44,750 --> 00:01:45,000
Grunting on both sides, but the lane east
is still open.

260
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:45,250
They are closing from above and below.
Keep east between them.

261
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:46,250
I see the ones around me. The floor east
is still clear.

262
00:01:46,250 --> 00:01:46,500
East again. Thread the gap before it
closes.

263
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:47,500
They are flanking me, but the next tile
east is clear.

264
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:47,750
A chair blocks the straight lane. Cut
northeast onto clear floor.

265
00:01:47,750 --> 00:01:48,000
One is southeast beside me. Cut northeast
again.

266
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Safe mode caught the step. I see them.
Northeast is still clear.

267
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Northeast now. Keep the space open.

268
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:50,250
Stairs ahead. Run north; the dead are
south and west.

269
00:01:50,250 --> 00:01:50,500
The stair edge is northwest. Get onto it.

270
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:51,500
I see them. The stairs are still my way
out.

271
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:52,500
Onto the stairs now.

272
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:53,500
Down into the basement. One narrow stair
is better than this open room.

273
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:53,750
Fire is spreading across the basement.
First, get away from the stairs.

274
00:01:53,750 --> 00:01:54,000
No one is below with me yet. Walk now and
save the lungs.

275
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Back to a walk. Breathe and listen.

276
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:56,000
South, away from the stairs and away from
the flames.

277
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
No footsteps down here. Keep south through
the dark.

278
00:01:58,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Keep south. The sofa is ahead, and the
fire is far to the east.

279
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Another quiet step south.

280
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:00,250
One more step south. Stop short of the
sofa until I judge the fire.

281
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:00,500
Look west before I step into the dark.

282
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:00,750
Check the tile immediately west.

283
00:02:00,750 --> 00:02:02,750
The carpet west is clear but offers no
cover. Stop looking.

284
00:02:02,750 --> 00:02:03,750
One shape north can see me. Move west,
away from the stairs and fire.

285
00:02:03,750 --> 00:02:04,750
Moaning south and southwest. Keep west
into the dark.

286
00:02:04,750 --> 00:02:05,750
A zombie northeast has found me. Move west
against the wall.

287
00:02:05,750 --> 00:02:07,750
The marker is gone. One careful step south
along the wall.

288
00:02:07,750 --> 00:02:08,000
One more step south. Keep the wall at my
back.

289
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:08,250
No eyes on me. Wait quietly and listen
before I try to sleep.

290
00:02:08,250 --> 00:02:08,500
No alarm. Just wait and listen.

291
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:08,750
Half an hour. Long enough to breathe,
short enough to hear trouble.

292
00:02:08,750 --> 00:02:09,000
The moaning is too close to sit still.
Stop waiting.

293
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Stop waiting now. Move before that thing
reaches me.

294
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Yes. Stop waiting. The zombie is too
close.

295
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:12,250
The wall holds west. Move south onto the
concrete.

296
00:02:12,250 --> 00:02:12,500
It has closed to my northeast. Run again.

297
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:12,750
Run south before it can grab me.

298
00:02:12,750 --> 00:02:13,750
South along the wall. Make it chase
instead of grab.

299
00:02:13,750 --> 00:02:14,750
A closed door is at my left. Open it and
get through.

300
00:02:14,750 --> 00:02:15,750
Open it west.

301
00:02:15,750 --> 00:02:16,750
Climb over the desk. Get west of the door.

302
00:02:16,750 --> 00:02:17,000
Shut the door behind me.

303
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:17,250
The door is shut and no eyes are on me.
Walk.

304
00:02:17,250 --> 00:02:18,250
Back to a walk. Listen at the door.

305
00:02:18,250 --> 00:02:20,250
Move west off the desk. See what this wall
was hiding.

306
00:02:20,250 --> 00:02:22,250
The door is taking blows. Move west toward
the bed.

307
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:22,500
More blows on the door. Get to the far end
of the bed.

308
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:22,750
On the bed, wait only a few breaths and
listen to the door.

309
00:02:22,750 --> 00:02:23,000
Just a short wait, no alarm.

310
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Twenty-four seconds. No more than that.

311
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:25,250
Yes. One more blow on the door is enough.

312
00:02:25,250 --> 00:02:25,500
The door still holds. Wait again, but
ignore the noise only, not real danger.

313
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:25,750
Wait, without setting an alarm.

314
00:02:25,750 --> 00:02:26,750
Thirty minutes, if the door keeps holding.

315
00:02:26,750 --> 00:02:36,750
The door is still shut. Ignore the sound
for now, but stay ready.

316
00:02:37,750 --> 00:02:47,750
Do not freeze here. Keep going.

317
00:02:48,750 --> 00:02:49,000
Yes. Stop waiting. The door is gone and
they are inside.

318
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:49,250
Look east through the breach before I
move.

319
00:02:49,250 --> 00:02:49,500
Check the bed tile east.

320
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:49,750
Check the next tile east.

321
00:02:49,750 --> 00:02:50,000
Look farther east, toward the breach.

322
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:50,250
Check the wall just north of the breach.

323
00:02:50,250 --> 00:02:50,500
Look down into the breach.

324
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:50,750
Smoke fills the doorway, and the slower
one cannot see me. Stop looking.

325
00:02:50,750 --> 00:02:51,000
The smoke hides me for now. Run south and
find another way out.

326
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:51,250
Run before the smoke thins.

327
00:02:51,250 --> 00:02:51,500
South over the stove, then around the
wardrobe.

328
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:52,500
I see the one east through the smoke.
South is still the path.

329
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:52,750
South now, while the smoke still buys
distance.

330
00:02:52,750 --> 00:02:53,000
Off the stove, farther south.

331
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:53,250
Still east of me. I need the south step.

332
00:02:53,250 --> 00:02:55,250
South now. Keep the wall between us as
long as I can.

333
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:56,250
The wardrobe blocks south. Cut southeast
around it.

334
00:02:56,250 --> 00:02:56,500
South is sealed concrete. Turn northwest,
back toward the bed.

335
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:58,500
One is east again. North is the only open
lane.

336
00:02:58,500 --> 00:02:59,500
North onto the bed. Keep the furniture
between us.

337
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:00,500
It is close southeast. Drop south and keep
circling.

338
00:03:00,500 --> 00:03:01,500
It is beside me to the east. South before
it grabs.

339
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:01,750
It has my right arm. Pull west against the
wall and tear free.

340
00:03:01,750 --> 00:03:02,000
Again. Tear the arm free.

341
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:02,250
More are east and northeast. I see them.
Keep pulling free.

342
00:03:02,250 --> 00:03:03,250
Pull west again. Get the arm loose.

343
00:03:03,250 --> 00:03:04,250
Arm free. Cut southeast before it grabs
again.

344
00:03:04,250 --> 00:03:04,500
It is north of me. Run east along the
south wall.

345
00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:04,750
Two flank me northwest and northeast. Run
between them before the gap closes.

346
00:03:04,750 --> 00:03:05,000
I see both of them. Keep the north gap.

347
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:06,000
North now. Slip between them.

348
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:07,000
They are on both sides. Keep north.

349
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Its hands miss. Keep north.

350
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:09,000
North is wall and one is south. Cut west
across the bed.

351
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,000
The bed slows me, but west opens space.

352
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:12,000
A groan comes from the east. Drop south
and keep circling.

353
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:13,000
One is beside me east again. South before
the grab.

354
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:14,000
The tough one has my torso. Pull west
against the wall.

355
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,000
Again. Tear free of the tough one.

356
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:15,250
It fell. Pull free once more.

357
00:03:15,250 --> 00:03:16,250
Keep pulling. Do not let it stand with
hold of me.

358
00:03:16,250 --> 00:03:16,500
Free. Cut southeast before both of them
stand over me.

359
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:16,750
One is north, one northeast. East along
the wall.

360
00:03:16,750 --> 00:03:17,750
A swimmer zombie is northeast. I see it.
East remains.

361
00:03:17,750 --> 00:03:18,750
East now, before it closes the step.

362
00:03:18,750 --> 00:03:19,000
It misses the grab. One step east.

363
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:19,250
East is wall now. Turn north past the one
northwest.

364
00:03:19,250 --> 00:03:20,250
North did not move me. Look at what blocks
it.

365
00:03:20,250 --> 00:03:21,250
Yes. Through the thin smoke. Standing
still is worse.

366
00:03:21,250 --> 00:03:21,500
The nearest one is west. Cut northeast
through the broken doorway.

367
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:21,750
Smoke is filling my lungs, and one is
west. East into the open room.

368
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:22,750
I see the shapes through the tears. East
is still the only way out.

369
00:03:22,750 --> 00:03:23,000
East now. Get out of the smoke.

370
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:23,250
The smoke is behind me. Run north toward
the closed door before the stairs.

371
00:03:23,250 --> 00:03:24,250
I see them east and north, still distant.
North to the door.

372
00:03:24,250 --> 00:03:25,250
North now. Reach the door before they
reach the corridor.

373
00:03:25,250 --> 00:03:25,500
North again through the corridor.

374
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:25,750
Keep north. The intact door is close.

375
00:03:25,750 --> 00:03:26,750
I see them east and north. The door is
still north.

376
00:03:26,750 --> 00:03:28,750
North now. Reach the intact door.

377
00:03:28,750 --> 00:03:29,750
One is beside me west. Keep north for the
door.

378
00:03:29,750 --> 00:03:30,750
The tough one has my right leg and the
wall is crushing me. Pull west.

379
00:03:30,750 --> 00:03:31,750
The northwest wall is empty. Brace against
it and wrench the leg free.

380
00:03:31,750 --> 00:03:32,750
Three holds on me. Brace against the
northwest wall and tear at all of them.

381
00:03:32,750 --> 00:03:33,750
All three still hold. Pull again.

382
00:03:33,750 --> 00:03:34,750
The tough one fell. Pull against the wall
again.

383
00:03:34,750 --> 00:03:35,750
My torso is free. The arm and leg are
still held. Pull again.

384
00:03:35,750 --> 00:03:36,750
My arm is free. The medic has my torso and
the tough one has my leg. Pull again.

385
00:03:36,750 --> 00:03:37,750
Another one has my left leg. Keep
wrenching against the wall.

386
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:38,750
My torso and both legs are held. Pull
again.

387
00:03:38,750 --> 00:03:39,750
My breath is giving out. The medic has my
torso and the tough one has my right leg.
Pull.

388
00:03:39,750 --> 00:03:40,750
My right leg is free. The medic still has
my torso. Pull again.

389
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:41,750
The smoke is dense and the medic still
holds my torso. Pull again.

390
00:03:41,750 --> 00:03:42,750
The medic still has me. Pull again through
the smoke.

391
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:43,750
I am bleeding and losing breath. The medic
still holds me. Pull.

392
00:03:43,750 --> 00:03:44,750
Still held, still breathing. Pull again.

393
00:03:44,750 --> 00:03:45,750
The medic still holds me. Pull again.

394
00:03:45,750 --> 00:03:46,750
My breath is fading, but the medic still
holds me. Pull again.

395
00:03:46,750 --> 00:03:47,000
The medic has my torso and another has my
right leg. Pull again.

396
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:47,250
The medic let go. Another has my torso and
right leg. Pull again.

397
00:03:47,250 --> 00:03:47,500
My body is breaking. The torso and right
leg are still held. Pull.

398
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:47,750
My last words should tell them they made
me fight.

399
00:03:47,750 --> 00:03:48,000
My last words should tell them they made
me fight.

400
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:48,250
My last words should tell them they made
me fight.

401
00:03:48,250 --> 00:03:48,500
My last words should tell them they made
me fight.

402
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:48,750
My last words should tell them they made
me fight.

403
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:49,000
My last words should tell them they made
me fight.

404
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:49,250
My last words should tell them they made
me fight.

405
00:03:49,250 --> 00:03:49,500
My last words should tell them they made
me fight.

406
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:49,750
My last words should tell them they made
me fight.

407
00:03:49,750 --> 00:03:50,000
My last words should tell them they made
me fight.

408
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:50,250
My last words should tell them they made
me fight.

409
00:03:50,250 --> 00:03:50,500
My last words should tell them they made
me fight.

410
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:50,750
My last words should tell them they made
me fight.

411
00:03:50,750 --> 00:03:51,000
My last words should tell them they made
me fight.

412
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:51,250
My last words should tell them they made
me fight.

413
00:03:51,250 --> 00:03:51,500
My last words should tell them they made
me fight.

414
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:51,750
Leave it there: you made me fight.

415
00:03:51,750 --> 00:03:52,000
There is nothing more for me to see.

416
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:52,250
Let the messages rest.

417
00:03:52,250 --> 00:03:52,500
No. My last words are enough.

418
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:52,750
